  "comments": [],
  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A spruce forest still stands upright underwater in Kazakhstan. Lake Kaindy, two thousand metres up in the Tian Shan, east of Almaty.",
        "visual_query": "tian shan mountain lake kazakhstan",
        "visual_fallback": "alpine mountain lake"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The trunks are Schrenk spruce. Branches and bark stripped away, bleached white, the tops breaking the surface like pale masts. Below, whole trees wait.",
        "visual_query": "submerged spruce trunks clear lake",
        "visual_fallback": "bare white trunks"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Cold mountain water keeps them from rotting. A limestone landslide dammed the gorge and drowned the valley. But when? Guidebooks all said 1911.",
        "visual_query": "limestone landslide mountain gorge",
        "visual_fallback": "rocky mountain gorge"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then researchers compared rings from the dead trunks against living spruce on the slopes above. The dead trees stopped growing after autumn 1888.",
        "visual_query": "tree ring core sample spruce",
        "visual_fallback": "tree rings closeup"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That points to the Chilik earthquake of July 1889, magnitude 8.2, twenty-two years earlier than anyone thought. The trees dated their own drowning.",
        "visual_query": "tian shan earthquake fault scarp",
        "visual_fallback": "mountain fault ridge"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
    "word_count": 115,
    "duration_seconds": 35.0,
